content.summary_of_the_review:
+ The problem of anti-forwarding is interesting; 

-  The problem of anti-forwarding is not properly defined: 

a) there is no way for the scanner to *stop* unauthorized forwarding as the paper claimed. First, the forwarding communication channels are not necessaarily public, making the scanner impossible to monitor such an unauthorized behavoir. Second, even if the forwarding on public communication channel is monitored, the scanner has no actionable capability to "stop" such a forwarding.   

b) without "stoping" funtionality, the "anti-forwarding" problem is a typical image watermarking problem in disguise. 

The problem statement should be revised accordingly; 

+ Experimental results are thorough and seem re-producible; 

-  Comparison with previous watermarking methods is not convincing: 

a) the requirement for previous watermarking methods that "a 100% message reconstruction can represent a successful anti-forwarding detection" is unfair and unnecessary, since a much lower message reconstruction bit rate is sufficient to claim the detection of suspect watermarks. 

b) The proposed method should be compared with previous watermarking methods with a reasonable reconstruction bit rate, that is comparable to the detection accuracy reported by the scanner;
